{"text": "Background\n==========\n\nMammalian sperm acquire most of their unique characteristics through their own evolutionary history as sperm inseminated females. Consequently, all species of the mammalian clade have evolved specialized spermatogenesis, which enables each species to develop and maintain a unique combination of sperm production parameters and sperm traits with respect to the function of each species. The evolutionary history of sperm production has been the subject of an increasing number of research efforts. This progress includes the identification of sperm types and genes that produce these sperm, their molecular evolution, as well as the evolutionary changes that they undergo during their reproductive career.\n\nThe evolutionary changes that sperm undergo before fertilizing an egg are mostly based on sperm\\'s own properties, such as sperm movement, motility, size, and shape. These morphological characteristics are under evolutionary selection to ensure that sperm are capable of fertilizing viable eggs within the female reproductive tract (sperm competition). The shape of the sperm is especially important, because shape is thought to be one of the most important determinants of sperm motility (egg penetration), where shape affects the trajectory of the sperm during the encounter with the egg. However, the evolutionary changes that sperm undergo throughout their lifetime are generally poorly understood.\n\nThe evolution of sperm motility\n------------------------------\n\nIn general, the evolution of sperm motility is associated with a series of evolutionary shifts that allow sperm to achieve its maximal energy potential. Motile sperm have evolved several strategies to increase their energy utilization. To enable high-speed

